Scope of the derivation
-----------------------

Rerobot contains no copied LeRobot source code. What is derived is the
*observable behaviour and public interface* of the following upstream modules,
taken from lerobot 0.6.1 at commit f37be3edbee60f3a09a5183788b91eb19f0c07d1:

  * src/lerobot/configs/types.py         -> rerobot-core::types
  * src/lerobot/types.py (TransitionKey) -> rerobot-core::types
  * src/lerobot/utils/action_interpolator.py
                                         -> rerobot-core::action_interpolator
  * src/lerobot/rollout/ring_buffer.py   -> rerobot-core::ring_buffer
  * src/lerobot/processor/rename_processor.py
                                         -> rerobot-core::processor::rename
  * src/lerobot/scripts/lerobot_info.py  -> rerobot-core::sysinfo, lerobot-info
  * pyproject.toml [project.scripts]     -> rerobot-compat::ENTRY_POINTS

Test expectations in this repository were derived in part from upstream's test
suite, in particular:

  * tests/policies/rtc/test_action_interpolator.py
  * tests/processor/test_rename_processor.py

The executable names lerobot-calibrate, lerobot-find-cameras, lerobot-find-port,
lerobot-record, lerobot-replay, lerobot-setup-motors, lerobot-teleoperate,
lerobot-eval, lerobot-train, lerobot-train-tokenizer, lerobot-dataset-viz,
lerobot-info, lerobot-find-joint-limits, lerobot-imgtransform-viz,
lerobot-edit-dataset, lerobot-setup-can, lerobot-annotate, and lerobot-rollout
are kept byte-identical to upstream's so that deployment tooling and
documentation continue to work. Commands that are not ported fail explicitly
rather than pretending to be their upstream counterparts; see
docs/compatibility.md.

